# Intro
A star in a periodic box of uniform gas that expands with the universe, at
a = 1 (redshift 0), with and without dust. A 3 Msun star sits at the centre of
a box of 32768 gas particles (1 atom/cm^3 physical, 5.12e8 Msun, 100 K, metal mass
fraction 1.54e-6). The box follows the Hubble expansion
for 180 Myr (a from 1 to about 1.0125). The gas state is frozen (cooling is off) and
no force acts: the example follows the light only.

The ISRF packet transport moves the light of the star through the gas. In an
expanding box the light loses energy to the expansion (it is redshifted) and
dust absorbs part of it. `leg` selects the box:

- `redshift`: expanding box, `GrackleCooling:local_dust_to_gas_ratio` set to
  1e-30, so no dust: the light only redshifts.
- `dust`: expanding box with the default dust-to-gas ratio (0.00939). Redshift
  and absorption act together.
- `static`: the same box and the same proper-time line without expansion: dust only.

`compareLegs.py` prints, per band, the fraction of the emitted energy that is
still in flight, absorbed, redshifted, or escaped, with the residual of the budget.
The PE band is the photoelectric heating band (6 to 11.2 eV) and the LW band the
Lyman-Werner band (11.2 to 13.6 eV). The energy lost to redshift of the LW band
is the signal of the expansion: it is zero in `static`. Comparing `dust`
with `redshift` and `static` shows how the two losses combine.

The time step is set by `n_steps`: SWIFT rounds the step down to a power of two
fraction of the run, so 2000 steps give 2048 steps (2049 rows in `timesteps.txt`) and every
step updates all the gas (one time bin). `Stars:max_timestep_young_Myr: 10` leaves the step of the star to the gas.
`GEARFeedback:ISRF_packet_light_speed` keeps its default (`cap`, 0.99 c). With
the default 2000 steps, a leg takes about an hour or more on 8 threads. Set `n_steps` and
`duration` smaller for a quick run, for example `n_steps=100 duration=0.0092`.

# Configure
```
./configure --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-feedback=GEAR --with-cooling=grackle_0 --with-stars=GEAR --with-sink=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-tracers=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-subgrid-radiation=all --enable-isrf-debugging-checks --with-chealpix=path/to/chealpix --with-grackle=path/to/grackle
make -j
```
`--with-chealpix` is required by the ISRF transport. `--enable-isrf-debugging-checks`
adds the `Packet*` snapshot fields that `compareLegs.py` reads. The number of
reception slots (the direction cells in which a gas particle stores the light it
receives) is the default of `--with-isrf-packet-slots` (12).

The example needs a radiation table whose `Data/Radiation` group carries
`MeanPhotonEnergyPE` and `Integrated_MeanPhotonEnergyPE`, besides the datasets
of the ISRF (section "The Data/Radiation group" of the stellar evolution
table documentation). The pychem table generator produces such a table from
its parameter file `params/chimieparam_radiation_parsec_spectral_popII.py`
(see the section "Where each Radiation field comes from" of the pychem page
"How to generate a Chemistry Table for GEAR"). No download is provided. Pass
the file with `radiation_table=/path/to/table.hdf5 ./run.sh`; the default is
`PopII_parsec_spectral_with_pe_photon_energy.hdf5` in this directory.

# Run
```
leg=redshift ./run.sh
leg=dust ./run.sh
leg=static ./run.sh
```

`run.sh` builds the initial conditions and runs swift in its own directory,
`run_<leg>`, which it empties first. Override a setting with an environment
variable: `n_threads`, `leg`, `redshift` (starting redshift of the expanding box),
`gas_density` (atom/cm^3, physical), `gas_mass` (Msun), `temperature` (K),
`star_mass` (Msun), `duration` (proper time in internal units, 0.184 is 180 Myr),
`n_steps`, `n_snapshots`, `swift_exe`, `run_dir` and `extra_args` (further swift
arguments). The metal mass fraction and the Hubble parameters are in `params.yml`.

# Compare
```
python3 compareLegs.py run_redshift run_dust run_static
```
